My guest today is Daryl Stone, Head of Product and Design at Citizen, the number one public safety app in the US.

8
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:57,520
Daryl defined and scaled Citizen's Consumer Subscription product with a dual focus on acquisition and retentive life saving features.

9
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:05,280
On the podcast, I talk with Daryl about going from zero to an eight figure ARR in just eighteen months.

10
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:11,200
Building a product improvement loop, combining user research, prototyping and AB testing.

11
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,840
And why expecting failure is one of the keys to success.

12
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:19,680
Hey, Daryl, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

13
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:21,840
Hey, David, thanks for having me.

14
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:23,840
Long time listener, second time caller.

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,360
I think the first time Jake Moore was talking about paywalls for thirty minutes and I didn't really want to stand in the way of that.

16
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:30,400
Yeah, I know.

17
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:33,840
So I invited you to be live on stage in New York City at Apple's Playhouse.

18
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:37,920
at Promotion Summit and then Jake and I like dominated the whole conversation.

19
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:43,840
But the whatever, you know, two or three minutes you got to talk, I just had so many follow-up questions.

20
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:47,520
So thanks for agreeing to come again so soon.

21
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:53,200
And so listeners who have listened to every episode will have heard your voice and gotten some of your insights.

22
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,000
But there's so much more that you've learned at Citizen.

23
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:58,080
We probably won't even cover half of it today.

24
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:00,320
So I'll have to have you back at some point.

25
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:06,880
But I did, I did want to kick things off just to understand why you joined Citizen.

26
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:18,400
So you were at Uber, it's an incredible company, it's grown tremendously over the past decade, and then you left this small kind of subscription startup that didn't actually monetize.

27
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:21,920
When I joined, we had no idea how we were going to monetize at Citizen.

28
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:24,720
That's a discussion I'm sure we'll get a lot more into.

29
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:28,960
But I think just for a quick background about myself, I run product and design at Citizen.

30
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:31,680
Now I joined as PM one back in 2019.

31
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:38,880
I'm our longest tenure product person at this point, three and a half years at C a startup is obviously a long time where I've learned quite a bit.

32
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:47,520
I think why I joined is actually really indicative of why I've stayed and, you know, withstood a lot of the adversity or just different challenges you face as you scale up a startup.

33
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:50,800
And, you know, I had amazing time at Uber, worked with incredible people.

34
00:02:50,960 --> 00:03:01,440
I was there from 2014 to 2019, first kind of on the early Uber pool team, kind of getting that product off the ground, very much a zero to one to many kind of opportunity, very mission driven.

35
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:04,880
You know, we were lowering the price of transportation to make it more accessible to people.

36
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:06,560
And we took that pretty far.

37
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:12,800
I started when it was in one city, got it to thirty cities, kind of was a core member of the product team and kind of on the leadership team there.

38
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:18,000
And then, you know, there was a pretty big cultural kind of shift at Uber and a lot of folks were looking around as to what to do next.

39
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:19,840
This was like 2017, 2018.

40
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:27,200
And I looked up and saw the driver app was kind of in serious need of some love and, you know, drivers around the world could really benefit from a better experience.

41
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:30,640
And so I joined one of my close friends and mentors, Yuki Yamashida.

42
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:34,800
Hey, Yuki, shout out to you if you're listening, who's now Chief Product Officer at Figma.

43
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:37,280
And him and I kind of co led, um, with him as the leader, kind of the.

44
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:40,560
I was the leader, kind of a redesign of the driver app, and we dismantled it to its basics.

45
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:44,640
We really thought deeply about the core experience and really making it great for drivers.

46
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:45,920
We dug in on user needs.

47
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:52,800
It was a amazing experience to build a product for someone that wasn't myself and really understand what was important to drivers.

48
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:55,920
And as part of that, I took a team to São Paulo, Brazil.

49
00:03:56,400 --> 00:03:57,520
We did a research trip there.

50
00:03:57,600 --> 00:03:59,440
We had data science and engineering.

51
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:00,800
We had like a mobile device lab.

52
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:07,600
We were driving around São Paulo in a bulletproof device lab, like really studying the Android devices and how they worked in different network conditions.

53
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:09,280
And we also talked to drivers.

54
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:10,320
We did user research.

55
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:15,520
And when we were talking to drivers, translated into Portuguese, you know, we wanted to know, how's this app we've been building?

56
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:16,080
Is it better?

57
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:17,120
What's, what's, what you like about it?

58
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:19,040
And they were like, yeah, yeah, yeah, it's great.

59
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:22,400
But, you know, we're getting robbed at KnifePoint with the cash we're collecting.

60
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:24,160
You know, what are you doing about our safety?

61
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:27,040
You know, our cars are breaking down in favelas.

62
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:29,360
What are you doing about our safety?

63
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:38,720
And so I left that trip just like with their feedback kind of burning into my brain and realizing, you know, safety was a massive and really unsolved or unsolved opportunity.

64
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:42,000
And I didn't feel like Uber was really in position A to solve it.

65
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:44,800
It was more like a social problem or challenge.

66
00:04:45,440 --> 00:04:48,960
And so fast forward, you know, three or four months, I had my second daughter.

67
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:53,280
I have two kids and I was looking around at other opportunities and I found Citizen.

68
00:04:53,840 --> 00:04:54,720
They were in two cities.

69
00:04:55,280 --> 00:05:02,320
They had a relatively immature but really, really strong kind of product in terms of the product market fit it had in places like New York.

70
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:08,320
And I was instantly captivated by Andrew, the founder, and the vision he had for a global network of people protecting each other.

71
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:14,160
And I believed in kind of the idea of it., I believed mobile technology could be used to protect people and create safety.

72
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:17,120
And so I joined as PM one to sort of build that out.

73
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:18,640
Yeah, that's amazing.

74
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,160
So why don't you tell us a little bit about Citizen the app?

75
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:30,080
I don't know that all of our listeners will actually be familiar with what Citizen does and how the app actually helps protect people.

76
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:32,080
Yeah, absolutely.

77
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:37,440
So what you should know about Citizen, first and foremost, is it's a moderated safety app.

78
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:49,680
So this isn't for those who aren't familiar with it or who are only casually familiar with it it, the way Citizen works is we actually send real-time emergency alerts of what first responders in your community are actively responding to.

79
00:05:50,160 --> 00:06:04,720
And we do that because we have a team of analysts, over a hundred, who are actually actively listening to police scanners, digitized police scanners in your community, listening for addresses and key pieces of information, and then providing instant real-time transparency as to what's going on.

80
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:14,640
So when you hear sirens going by, you know, you can be aware and extra vigilant as a citizen as to what's happen our free product.

81
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:17,760
It's free in thirty, forty major cities now in the US.

82
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:30,160
And what's really powerful about that product or that application is it creates through this instant transparency opportunities for citizens to stay safer, to exit burning buildings when there's a fire in their building, even when their alarms are not working.

83
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:34,080
We actually beat fire alarms in some places in New York or older apartments where they're failing.

84
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:39,280
We have stories of people exiting buildings they didn't realize were on fire because of a notification they got on their phone.

85
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:39,680
Wow.

86
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:53,360
It helps you avoid active shooting situations or potential fireally dangerous situations where reports of weapons or actual observed weapons are kind of exposed around you and people are using that information in real time to just make safer decisions for themselves and their family.

87
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:54,960
And it also helps people.

88
00:06:55,840 --> 00:07:11,360
You know, you if your dog runs away or your child is missing or kidnapped, we have many cases where notifications sent to a broad community of people in near real time has resulted in the community coming to their aid and actually helping people and creating kind of real time community safety.

89
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:20,720
That's the become part of the kind of transportation or, I should say, the safety infrastructure of the major cities that we operate in.

90
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:26,240
I was going to say, and I shared this anecdote before, but it really was a fantastic experience.

91
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:34,560
When we first met in New York City, of course, you know, I live in a small town and even live in a neighborhood that doesn't see much action.

92
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:36,880
So I had never downloaded the app.

93
00:07:37,120 --> 00:07:39,440
When I flew into New York, the first thing I did was download the app.

94
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:44,640
And sure enough, that first night there were sirens outside my hotel and there was like some active.

95
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:52,560
And when I heard all the sirens, you know, the first instinct is, oh, do I, you know, look out my window?

96
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:54,240
Do I pop out and see what's going on?

97
00:07:54,800 --> 00:08:00,880
And, you know, I pulled up the citizen app and it told me exactly what was going on and it was clearly safer for me to stay in the hotel room.

98
00:08:01,360 --> 00:08:09,840
And so I didn't go out that night and it was, it was genuinely a great experience for someone, you know, coming from a small town and then like knowing kind of what to do in that situation.

99
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:16,800
Yeah, it's a very unique product and, you know, the free app has basically always grown virally since it's been introducedced.

100
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:25,200
The real challenge and what I sort of stepped in to help solve is, so, you know, what is a paid offering on top of this really powerful, extremely valuable free app?

101
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:26,320
Like, what does that look like?

102
00:08:26,640 --> 00:08:28,080
And it's been a journey.

103
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:29,840
You know, we didn't know when I joined.

104
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:31,680
It took us some time even through COVID.

105
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:34,000
We can talk more about that to figure out what it might look like.

106
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:40,560
But what we sort of landed on is, you know, you have these agents, hundreds of agents who are kind of moderating your community safety for free.

107
00:08:40,880 --> 00:08:46,400
Our paid app, Citizen Premium, effectively allows you to reach those same agents to get real time.

108
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:56,480
And, you know, it's really powerful when you open up that direct channel to, you know, a trusted third party, like citizen who's trained and incentived to just keep you safe.

109
00:08:56,960 --> 00:08:57,680
You know, what can happen?

110
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:00,240
It's a first of its kind kind of safety offering.

111
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:04,960
What's really powerful about it is it integrates all your existing kind of emergency response.

112
00:09:04,960 --> 00:09:09,920
We can call 911 and send them to your location better than, you know, most mobile devices.

113
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,240
If you call 911, what happens is the first minute is basically trying to figure out where you are.

114
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:18,240
With us, we can route responders to exactly where you are, which is incredibly powerful.

115
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:21,520
But even bigger than that, you can text us.

116
00:09:21,840 --> 00:09:28,320
We have kind of silent capabilities that allow you to get instant second set of eyes of digital witness or guardian, if you will.

117
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:31,200
And you can reach us before something escalates.

118
00:09:31,440 --> 00:09:37,440
There's this really interesting kind of artifact of the emergency response system where it's like, you don't call it unless it's an emergency or something.

119
00:09:37,680 --> 00:09:49,200
And we actually view preventative safety and the idea that you can proactively address when you feel uneasy and take an action that actually ensures your safety and navigates you to safety with, you know, your own safety.

120
00:09:49,440 --> 00:10:04,720
Someone who's just instantly by your side, making sure you get home safely, making sure your Uber arrives on time, making sure you can find your wits if you're in an unfamiliar area and just get somewhere where there's an open place to kind of catch your breath and really make sure you're safe.

121
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:13,440
We've seen that service as a subscription service just have a really powerful type of product market fit and create a really powerful type of consumer experience.

122
00:10:13,760 --> 00:10:28,320
Most of us have never seen anywhere, which is like truly kind of a rescue or a safety intervention where our subscribers' lives are dramatically different because they had the app on their phone and were able to reach us before something escalated or while something was escalating to get life saving help.

123
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:29,840
That's incredible.

124
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:42,000
And it's great kind of context since I think a lot of our listeners will probably have just heard very, you know, just news headlines about citizens not like actually experiencing the app or kind of understanding the value prop.

125
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:53,680
And so as we talk through citizen strategy and citizen monetization and all the things you're working on, kind of having that as a backdrop helps understand why things are built a certain way.

126
00:10:54,400 --> 00:11:13,280
And so with that in mind, I actually wanted to transition to talking about that transition to freemium, where you did actually start charging because there's there's going to be a lot of tension between the kind of mission of keeping people more safe and then actually charging money to keep them more safe.

127
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:21,280
And I'm sure that's kind of a daily tension of like, how do we actually help the most people we possibly can, but then also as a for profit business.

128
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:24,880
business actually make money helping them do that?

129
00:11:25,920 --> 00:11:26,000
Yeah.

130
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:30,080
And the way we think about it, it's a great it's a great tension.

131
00:11:30,240 --> 00:11:39,920
It's a great observation, but like the way we think about it is, you know, the the free product has basically transformed the way that the public alerting system works in the communities we operate in.

132
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:57,920
Communities are fundamentally different with sort of citizen creating real-time transparency and helping people make better and more informed decisions in a micro kind of on a micro basis in certain communities, you know, where there is sort of the threat of violence or even just where there's a safety like event in progress.

133
00:11:58,880 --> 00:12:00,800
So that's already been sort of transformational.

134
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:31,920
And we kind of think about our premium offering, Citizen Premium, as almost the next phase of the company where we transition from simply making sure you're aware of what is of what's going on and can kind of make better decisions with that information to actively participating in your safety directly and ensuring you have access to the best possible type of help that's ever been created, which we really believe is being built on Citizen aggregating existing response types and having a sort of citizen agent who's highly trained in the middle of it with all the powers of kind of mobile technology at your fingertips.

135
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:37,120
And it just turns out to build something like that actually requires a sort of transactional model.

136
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:38,800
You can't give that away for free.

137
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:40,640
It starts to stop making sense.

138
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:44,080
And many of us joined for the mission of safety.

139
00:12:44,480 --> 00:12:53,600
And what we've realized is you start by basically ensuring a group of people have access to something that's better for their safety than anything else that exists.

140
00:12:54,160 --> 00:13:06,240
And then as a phase two, almost, you democratize that access, find ways to do give one get ones, find different partnerships or avenues that give people access to this incredible kind of transformative safety product we're building.

141
00:13:06,720 --> 00:13:19,440
But you do so in a way that perpetuates the existence of your company because that's how you kind of perpetuate safety indefinitely and not kind of devolve into a non profit or something that actually would probably pretty quickly lose steam.

142
00:13:19,920 --> 00:13:39,760
And so what we're chasing and kind of what we've been building is, I think, one of the few examples of kind of a free product with a direct network effect that's kind of viral in nature with social sharing and content kind of distributing through communities in real time, but having a kind of monetization engine attached to that that actually amplifies the quality of the free offering.

143
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:52,080
It's a little bit of a different type of business to scale than I think some of the other subscriptions you've had on your platform where they effectively already know kind of day one, okay, we'll hook this in.

144
00:13:52,240 --> 00:13:53,520
This is our monetization engine.

145
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:55,280
We'll drive traffic from these channels.

146
00:13:55,600 --> 00:13:56,800
We're not really operating that way.

147
00:13:57,120 --> 00:14:11,600
We've actually been a lot more, I would say, discovery based almost in how we've kind of found this model and figured out how to monetize this free product in a way that ensures a level of safety for free, but obviously advanced protection if you're able to subscribe.

148
00:14:12,160 --> 00:14:24,800
Yeah, and it's interesting because we've talked before about how you really are kind of building the ultimate freemium model and you have the financing and the team in place to go after this.

149
00:14:25,360 --> 00:14:36,960
For a lot of smaller apps, freemium can be really tricky because the more value you give away, are you really going to convert those subscribers over time or are you just like, you know, just giving it away?

150
00:14:37,600 --> 00:14:46,800
And looking at stats, some developer friends shared on Twitter, you know, something like 92 percent of anyone who ever subscribes subscribes within that first day.

151
00:14:47,920 --> 00:15:02,400
But I think Citizen is in a really different place that, like, as you continue to add, you kind of benefit and get value out of there being more people on the platform and you further the mission of giving that value away for free.

152
00:15:02,880 --> 00:15:19,040
And then over time, there is opportunity to, you know, when something happens in a community, when something happens at an individual level, to actually kind of build on that free offering over time to actually monetize more and more of your customers over time.

153
00:15:19,840 --> 00:15:29,280
So I think it, you know, it's an incredible example of how freemium can work incredibly well and that there is an opportunity to monetize after you've built.

154
00:15:29,920 --> 00:15:32,720
a huge freemium base.

155
00:15:33,120 --> 00:15:33,200
Yeah.

156
00:15:33,360 --> 00:15:42,640
And it's a really important observation you made, which is our product actually has a network effect in that it's better when your neighbors are on it and when people in your community are also participating.

157
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:50,000
People can add video to incidents, they can comment and provide useful tips or pieces of real-time awareness in a very local way.

158
00:15:50,320 --> 00:16:00,480
These are people who are because of our usage of location, you know, within a few blocks of you who have the right level of content, context to kind of be able to help you in a real-time situation.

159
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:14,480
And we're at the beginning of kind of deepening the premium offerings connection to the free product and in many ways unlocking what we believe is an incredible opportunity, which is more kind of active participation in community safety.

160
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:23,520
An example of this we haven't shared that I'll share with you is we carried a test in Soho, New York, where we simply sent a notification that said citizen user needs EpiPen.

161
00:16:23,760 --> 00:16:26,480
And within thirty seconds, we had notified a thousand people.

162
00:16:26,560 --> 00:16:29,200
We had five people in comments saying, I have an EpiPen.

163
00:16:29,600 --> 00:16:30,160
Where should I go?

164
00:16:30,320 --> 00:16:30,800
How can I help?

165
00:16:31,920 --> 00:17:01,760
We are not rushing to productionize this quite yet, but we believe the nearby network and the connection of the community to safety is almost like the ultimate marketplace opportunity in that if you can index the skills and capabilities in a trusted way of people in your community and put them in a position where they can help in a supplemental way to the existing emergency response system, but in a very meaningful one if you think about medical cases where seconds matter, that's the opportunity that we see ahead of us.

166
00:17:01,920 --> 00:17:13,040
And we're really the only show in town in terms of really focusing on personal and community safety and having all of the pieces in place to build something of transformational and life saving value for communities.

167
00:17:13,120 --> 00:17:14,560
And that's what keeps us going.

168
00:17:14,880 --> 00:17:16,160
That's what keeps us motivated.

169
00:17:16,400 --> 00:17:22,640
And we're really kind of when you zoom out day zero in terms of what the opportunity is and what we've been building.

170
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:23,920
Yeah.

171
00:17:24,320 --> 00:17:41,600
And then, you know, another amazing thing about the subscription model and actually having your users become customers and pay via subscription is that you're not incentivized to do a lot of the creepy things that some that a company in your position might actually do.

172
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:47,360
So we were talking kind of chatting before we hit record about how you don't sell location data.

173
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:57,120
And I think a lot, you know, a lot of people would expect that just given how a lot of social networks and even weather apps and other apps have got a bad rap for doing that.

174
00:17:57,840 --> 00:18:07,760
But by actually charging your users, you're incentivized to not do that because it's a better experience for a customer knowing that their private data and their most sensitive data is not being shared.

175
00:18:08,160 --> 00:18:09,360
Yeah, that's exactly right.

176
00:18:09,600 --> 00:18:35,440
And if I could give, you know, a word of advice to your people in your audience who might be evaluating different opportunities and kind of trying to figure out what they want to do with their career, I would say more than anything, more than, you know, what's published and the cultural values or whatever, look at how a company's business model works really, really closely because how a company makes their money will dictate their actual kind of mode of operating and their actual kind of cultural values.

177
00:18:35,920 --> 00:18:51,680
And I think, you know, the ad based cell data for, you know, different types of access model creates a lot of perverse incentives for consumers and does, I think, erode, you know, trust and privacy and really, perhaps even safety if you zoom out loud enough.

178
00:18:52,400 --> 00:19:01,120
And it's really important to us at Citizen that, you know, we monetize in a very transparent way with a very clear value exchange from our users and the services that are rendered.

179
00:19:01,600 --> 00:19:04,880
And that's why we don't sell data and we never plan to and never intend to.

180
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:06,240
Yeah, that's amazing.

181
00:19:06,960 --> 00:19:18,400
And speaking of plans, you're sharing a little bit about so taking Citizen Global is going to be a huge challenge, but also a very, I imagine, exciting challenge internally.

182
00:19:19,200 --> 00:19:22,480
So I'd I'd love to hear a little bit more about Citizen at a global scale.

183
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:26,080
Yeah, so I think I mentioned this to you before the podcast.

184
00:19:26,320 --> 00:19:27,840
You know, we have an initiative underway right now.

185
00:19:28,080 --> 00:19:29,360
We're calling Citizen Global.

186
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:44,560
And I think part of the journey we've been on as a company is really moving from sort of an inspired proof of concept to one of the, you know, best MVPs almost ever released, kind of, if you look back on how it grew virally in New York and other places, thanks to our founders' incredible vision and product sense.

187
00:19:44,720 --> 00:19:51,040
Andrew's a amazing product thinker and operator, but really graduating that to a mature business with a path to scale.

188
00:19:51,520 --> 00:19:54,640
And that's been the journey we've been on for the past few years.

189
00:19:54,880 --> 00:20:03,040
And obviously, solving monetization or figuring out our monetization story and being able to make extreme traction has been a core part of that journey.

190
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:11,840
And as we kind of appraise where we're at, and we're starting to bring in better advisors and just really connect the narrative of our free and paid products together.

191
00:20:12,320 --> 00:20:19,440
We're realizing, you know, we actually have a pretty convincing story for how this goes global, how this kind of helps the driver, the Uber drivers in São Paulo.

192
00:20:19,600 --> 00:21:00,160
And the way that we sort of view the core product scaling is actually, you know, we're a moderated safety marketplace powered by consumer subscriptions where we'll index the local types of help in your communities, you know, the different emergency response networks and perhaps volunteer organizations and groups who are already helping on the ground and would help in more ways if they had better better access to information and who needs help and powering that through, you know, a subscription product that sort of functions the way citizen does today where, you know, incidents are created, the community is alerted, help is sort of happening on those incidents and through those incidents in some cases where first response and private members of the community can coordinate and work together to create safety.

193
00:21:01,120 --> 00:21:25,040
And, you know, we believe that that will be the product we bring to London, to São Paulo, to these different places around the world and sort of launch, if you will, something much closer to like a safety marketplace almost with a high focus on moderation and a high focus on preventative safety, like before things escalate because of this like deep connection between the community and the helpers and responders functionally operating as a kind of a deterrent.

194
00:21:25,680 --> 00:21:27,520
And so that's what we're building out right now.

195
00:21:27,600 --> 00:21:31,680
We're kind of actively reorienting our entire product around our consumer subscription.

196
00:21:32,160 --> 00:21:45,760
It's a little bit disconnected if you use the current version, but we're sprinting across a set of milestones with Sean Rad from Tinder, who has been a very involved advisor who's become a really a trusted thought partner of mine and loves the product and the direction of it.

197
00:21:46,640 --> 00:21:48,640
And that's sort of what we're building out right now.

198
00:21:48,960 --> 00:22:08,560
And Sean cares, I don't want to put words in his mouth, but he's excited because he's seen sort of the Israeli-Palestine conflict and these real kind of intense safety situations where there's just an obvious opportunity for a better layer of consumer software to create safety and transparency by better digitizing and democratizing information.

199
00:22:08,720 --> 00:22:10,880
And that's sort of exactly what we're building here.

200
00:22:11,040 --> 00:22:17,760
And we're sort of the only share show in town, as I've said, which makes this a pretty exciting opportunity when you look at it from the right aperture.

201
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:33,280
You may not have gotten this far, so feel free to punt if you don't have a coherent answer yet or kind of still in investigation phases, but something I get asked a lot is how do you price globally?

202
00:22:33,520 --> 00:22:39,040
So for a lot of subscription apps, the US ends up making up a vast majority of the revenue.

203
00:22:39,520 --> 00:22:43,200
And then, of course, then you hit some of the bigger countries like, you know, China.

204
00:22:43,200 --> 00:22:50,240
Like, you know, the UK and Germany and Japan are typically in the top, you know, eight countries for monetization on a subscription app.

205
00:22:51,200 --> 00:23:05,360
But when you talk about moving into Brazil and to other countries like that, have you all started researching the opportunity there financially and like how the pricing will be different, how the cost will be different?

206
00:23:06,080 --> 00:23:08,480
Is that something actively being worked on?

207
00:23:09,840 --> 00:23:13,280
We're still very early in how the service will look and localize.

208
00:23:14,720 --> 00:23:19,040
I think I can speak more on almost the philosophical side of it or kind of what's in our toolkit.

209
00:23:19,200 --> 00:23:26,320
You know, I think we'll obviously do, I think conjoint surveys and kind of willingness to pay surveys are a pretty solid foundational, you know, tool.

210
00:23:26,480 --> 00:23:30,400
If you're not familiar with them, I would encourage, you know, folks that are listening to look those up.

211
00:23:31,360 --> 00:23:48,560
Also, I think, you know, rapid and iterative testing is a huge element of kind of developing a sort of a strong sense of what solid ground is versus what is speculative and something I push my teams to do and really I push our company to to do is tie break with data and with testing as much as possible.

212
00:23:49,760 --> 00:23:57,760
You may have noticed I said citizen premium, but if you google, you'll see there's actually a lot of citizen protect is kind of what we call our subscription currently.

213
00:23:57,920 --> 00:24:06,880
And the reason we're rebranding in part is we did some paywall testing and found that citizen premium on top of super wall was actually converting and retaining a little bit better.

214
00:24:07,040 --> 00:24:10,560
And we thought it actually was a more clarifying way of describing the product.

215
00:24:10,960 --> 00:24:15,520
And we make our decisions kind of with a blend of intuition and data.

216
00:24:15,520 --> 00:24:22,000
And that's just an example of that that I think would apply to our kind of global go to market, if you will.

217
00:24:22,640 --> 00:24:38,480
And speaking of that, you recently hired a user full time user research person with an assistant, which, I mean, at your scale, that's more feasible than for a lot of smaller subscription apps.

218
00:24:39,360 --> 00:24:46,320
But I think a lot of even the smaller subscription apps probably rely a little too heavily just on that quantifiable data.

219
00:24:46,400 --> 00:24:49,840
data and doing AB tests and that kind of thing.

220
00:24:50,320 --> 00:25:05,360
But tell me about this user research role and how it shapes even how you perform and evaluate AB tests and then how you also incorporate that kind of intuitive understanding of the user into the decision making and kind of product development.

221
00:25:06,240 --> 00:25:11,760
Yeah, I would say we've done two things this year that have materially improved our product development process.

222
00:25:12,080 --> 00:25:14,480
The first was, as you said, hiring a user researcher.

223
00:25:14,800 --> 00:25:17,360
The second was actually activating a kind of prototyping.

224
00:25:18,160 --> 00:25:18,480
engineer.

225
00:25:18,640 --> 00:25:22,480
We kind of converted one of our really talented animators to a prototyping engineer.

226
00:25:22,720 --> 00:25:27,360
And those two kinds of working in concert have just been an incredible unlock for us as a company.

227
00:25:27,520 --> 00:25:50,880
And I don't know how familiar people are with user research, but I would say the benefit of user research is it just gives a much more systematic and thorough voice to your customer base or your potential customer base because you have someone owning that as kind of a dedicated discipline versus this kind of thing you do on an ad hoc basis when you're looking to validate or potentially confirmation bias, you know, something that you think is a good idea.

228
00:25:51,200 --> 00:25:58,880
And for us, where we have a lot of internal conviction, we have people who have been building for years and really kind of believe, have deeply held views on what probably will work.

229
00:25:59,200 --> 00:26:06,960
It's actually been a really helpful tie break to activate a more independent, you know, voice of the customer throughout our process.

230
00:26:07,360 --> 00:26:13,760
And that manifests in surveys and research interviews to just build a kind of a foundational understanding of our customer base.

231
00:26:14,240 --> 00:26:19,920
A big initiative we did earlier this year was identify the seven core needs that, you know, we believe citizens have.

232
00:26:19,920 --> 00:26:25,440
We believe Citizen needs to meet in order to really kind of achieve a global safety position.

233
00:26:25,600 --> 00:26:29,360
And those key needs and meeting them is part of our language for developing.

234
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:37,200
And then where that really collides is we put together higher fidelity prototypes of things that we think are worth building.

235
00:26:37,360 --> 00:27:02,880
And before we just sprint to push something to production, we actually show them to users and demystify some of the things that you would learn months later when you ship something and find out, oh man, it turns out they didn't press that button or it turns out everyone didn't get that screen and that feedback loop has created a much more rapid and iterative approach to our development, which is starting to really improve our hit rate on kind of newer products where there is inherently a margin of error to building and releasing them.

236
00:27:03,360 --> 00:27:05,360
And how has that changed AB testing?

237
00:27:05,920 --> 00:27:13,200
So you're prototyping and you kind of have a general understanding maybe of how things are going to hit in production.

238
00:27:13,920 --> 00:27:28,560
Do you still roll those prototyped and kind of validated experiences out into the production app as an AB test or do you kind of roll them out with a level of conviction that you wouldn't have otherwise without having done the process?

239
00:27:29,760 --> 00:27:31,440
We definitely still AB test.

240
00:27:31,760 --> 00:27:38,960
I mean, I think there's a version of like the product, Hippocratic Oath, you know, first do no harm that I've that I've sort of long adhered to.

241
00:27:39,360 --> 00:27:51,040
No matter how well you think something is built or conceived, you always want to roll out and first do no harm and kind of build on a base that's, you know, solid and established while you continue to iterate.

242
00:27:51,840 --> 00:28:00,400
What I really push my teams towards is, you know, I call it milestones, not cliffs, where we don't want to over build something and release it without follow on plan.

243
00:28:00,640 --> 00:28:12,720
We want to release it kind of early in that first, you know, harm phase, have plans for progressive enhancements and take the time to invest beyond the starting line to progressively enhance something to the point where it can achieve kind of a metric goal.

244
00:28:13,680 --> 00:28:36,240
I think less mature product companies will, you know, assume that this first release solves all problems and quickly find out that a bad first release can createate far more problems than it solves if you're not really thorough in terms of how you roll it out, how you learn from the ecosystem you've just destabilized or the user experience you've just kind of destabilized.

245
00:28:36,560 --> 00:28:40,240
And we design and build and release with that very much in mind.

246
00:28:40,960 --> 00:28:43,200
So then what does that loop look like?

247
00:28:43,600 --> 00:28:59,680
So user research identifies a customer need, prototyper prototypes an experience that goes out to real users of the app in coordination with the user researcher to actually look at those things.

248
00:29:00,400 --> 00:29:02,320
And then that rolls out as an AB test.

249
00:29:03,040 --> 00:29:06,240
And then you're looking at the actual production response.

250
00:29:06,480 --> 00:29:09,840
And then that data feeds back to both the researcher and the product team.

251
00:29:10,400 --> 00:29:13,920
Like, I mean, I'm kind of pulling it all together based on what you said.

252
00:29:14,160 --> 00:29:16,480
Yeah, that's that's actually more or less how it works.

253
00:29:16,640 --> 00:29:16,720
Yeah.

254
00:29:16,960 --> 00:29:22,640
And I think a key question for startup product leaders is really like how much can you parallelize?

255
00:29:23,200 --> 00:29:24,480
Because you don't want to have too many.

256
00:29:26,160 --> 00:29:40,160
steps that are matrixed and dependent so that you're basically like lines down on a core function like engineering who could be making progress on foundational components that are quote unquote locked even while you're validating sort of like the things on the margin.

257
00:29:40,640 --> 00:29:56,160
I'm curious in describing that process, it kind of reminded me of something you shared when we were talking about potential topics for the for the podcast is that one of the top things you recommend to product folks is to listen to any.

258
00:29:56,160 --> 00:30:00,960
to Annie Duke on the Knowledge Project and I actually love her book Thinking and Betts.

259
00:30:01,760 --> 00:30:13,360
And so I'd I'd love to hear kind of your thoughts on how that thinking inspires the way you approach product development and why you recommend it to anyone working on the product team.

260
00:30:14,320 --> 00:30:20,080
So the name of that podcast, I think it's like the best name of a podcast I've heard is actually getting better at being wrong.

261
00:30:21,040 --> 00:30:31,840
And I share that because when you're developing new products and when you're working kind of earlier stage consumer environments, you're just inevitably going to be wrong a portion of the time.

262
00:30:32,160 --> 00:30:33,200
There's too many decisions.

263
00:30:33,440 --> 00:30:38,240
There's too many things that you have to like that are just gotcha in the course of building something new.

264
00:30:38,960 --> 00:31:04,240
And it's just as important when you're leading a team through that process that they understand sort of the true kind of nature of the game and that there isn't an expectation of a hundred percent hit rate or getting everything right moment one, but rather that we have such a shared long term viewpoint of what we're building together that we can withstand any specific sort of bumpster bruises along the way and really building kind of antifragility and resilience into your process.

265
00:31:04,880 --> 00:31:08,960
And what Annie Duke talks about is really structuring everything basically as a bet.

266
00:31:09,360 --> 00:31:32,080
And I think when you, when you liberate yourself from being right and acknowledge there's an uncertainty underlying what you're doing, it's a bet, you can start to think about the expected value of that bet and how to sort of tweak the effort to impact ratio or tradeoff to get the same expected value for less effort, which is a great way to sort of derisk that bet or to get way more expected value with a little more effort., which is a great way to sort of structure that better, that thing.

267
00:31:32,880 --> 00:31:57,600
And when you talk about everything in terms like that and acknowledge the uncertainty, and it's incredibly powerful when you're talking to engineers or designers or even executives, when you use words like, you know, we believe this to be the best path because or, you know, we think this is going to work because of these things, but, and acknowledge sort of the implicit uncertainty, you just create a lot more of an intellectually honest environment for development.

268
00:31:57,840 --> 00:32:01,360
You create instant kind of psychological safety and trust throughout your team.

269
00:32:01,760 --> 00:32:06,720
Because when you've been at a startup for years, you've seen, you know, the metrics that have moved a ton and those that have stayed kind of flat.

270
00:32:06,960 --> 00:32:11,200
And at a certain point it becomes dishonest if everything is going to be the next big thing that wins big.

271
00:32:11,600 --> 00:32:20,400
But it's much more important that everyone shares the same language for how they talk about what they're doing and they all understand what it will feel like or look like when you do win big.

272
00:32:20,800 --> 00:32:36,080
And that's sort of, I think, what I coach my team on that like getting better at being wrong and how you sort of lead teams to become antifragile and to ship things that have a really believable chance to change the game and win, but doing so in a way that creates long term cultural continuity.

273
00:32:37,040 --> 00:32:37,120
Yeah.

274
00:32:37,760 --> 00:32:45,680
And I imagine over the last eighteen months getting citizens revenue engine off the ground, you were wrong a lot.

275
00:32:46,320 --> 00:32:58,160
And so I'm curious if there's any specific lessons you could share from that experience of both things you got wrong and things you feel like you got right over the last year or eighteen months.

276
00:32:58,320 --> 00:33:08,400
And, you know, it's no small accomplishment to have gone from from zero and a completely free product to now, uh, eight figures in ARR.

277
00:33:08,560 --> 00:33:11,200
It's an incredible accomplishment in such a short amount of time.

278
00:33:11,840 --> 00:33:22,320
Uh, so I imagine you all have, have talked about things and learned lessons, uh, and made mistakes that a lot of folks would benefit from, from, from hearing some of that experience.

279
00:33:23,360 --> 00:33:23,840
Absolutely.

280
00:33:24,080 --> 00:33:31,040
And I think maybe I'd start by giving, sharing almost the tips that I think have really helped us be right more than we've been wrong.

281
00:33:31,200 --> 00:33:35,920
And I can through that, I think it will be clear where we sort of what was sort of a unlock for us or a real value add.

282
00:33:36,320 --> 00:33:56,800
And the first tip I would give is kind of really focusing on your top of funnel and kind of what is driving your growth engine or your feedback loops, because it's really important you understand kind of the psychology of the people who are entering your kind of core app experience, you know, what types of content have they been shared, what ads did they see if you're doing paid.

283
00:33:56,960 --> 00:33:58,400
We didn't do paid for a very long time.

284
00:33:58,720 --> 00:34:02,880
And just kind of like, you know, who are they and kind of like what what what what what what what what is their kind of makeup and mentality?

285
00:34:02,960 --> 00:34:06,240
That's by far, I think, one of the most important things to really focus on.

286
00:34:07,360 --> 00:34:17,520
Then, you know, once you've kind of got a clear understanding of what's driving that loop and ideally some way of consistently driving it, because you need a flow of people to even know how to operate on and scale your product.

287
00:34:18,240 --> 00:34:24,480
And, you know, that starts with like the seed idea, which is a core product that is valuable and does is marketable or does kind of grow itself.

288
00:34:25,120 --> 00:34:34,720
The next kind of core thing to really focus on is, you know, that really that first session to perhaps seven day journey, but first session matters almost.

289
00:34:34,960 --> 00:34:47,520
almost ten x as much as I think for seven days in terms of really having a specific moment, which is your onboarding experience, where there's a shared understanding from the user and the developer or the team that this is onboarding.

290
00:34:47,920 --> 00:34:49,520
You can communicate differently in that moment.

291
00:34:49,840 --> 00:35:04,000
You can present things in that moment that you really don't want them to miss and ensure they have the right level of context and inspiration to kind of take the plunge with you and try this new thing they've maybe never heard of or don't deeply or fully understand the value of yet.

292
00:35:04,720 --> 00:35:15,520
That is sort of your golden momentden moment to really tell your story well and iterating on that experience, running tests on that experience that move a metric surprisingly up with a position that really resonates.

293
00:35:16,080 --> 00:35:22,240
We've seen video has been incredibly helpful for us for paywalls because our product requires a little bit of like visual description.

294
00:35:23,120 --> 00:35:33,120
This idea of an agent who's with you and can walk you home or who you can text anywhere all the time with whatever you might be concerned about, that's a concept that's a little bit new.

295
00:35:33,680 --> 00:35:35,280
It's not like a different mattress or something.

296
00:35:36,240 --> 00:35:48,640
So really kind of understanding the psychology and that first kind of onboarding moment and building your onboarding very modularly so you can in a very lightweight way test and iterate on it without sort of waiting a month for your feedback loop.

297
00:35:49,600 --> 00:35:54,320
That is an incredibly important kind of engine to sort of get off the ground in the early days.

298
00:35:54,560 --> 00:36:07,760
I mean, we'll talk about tooling soon, but Superwall, the paywall kind of AB testing tool that Jake Moore, who was on your podcast, is the founder of, was an incredible unlock for us because we could iterate so much faster on a very core part of our value presentation.

299
00:36:08,160 --> 00:36:13,520
The third tip I would say, and this is kind of related to the second, is have a really strong understanding of buy versus build.

300
00:36:14,240 --> 00:36:19,040
I think in the early days we sort of probably overbuilt, if you will, some of the key components.

301
00:36:19,520 --> 00:36:22,960
We inhoused some things that there was a tool for we could have used off the shelf.

302
00:36:23,120 --> 00:36:29,760
And I think what we didn't realize in making that tradeoff of buy versus build was you get a lot more than just the tool when you buy.

303
00:36:30,080 --> 00:36:35,120
You get sort of industry experience and perspectives of people who are now rooting for you.

304
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:47,440
You also reduce your long term maintenance costs and get the opportunity to for more of your team to be focused on value versus maintenance, which if you're a startup, you want everyone doing value and almost no one doing maintenance unless they're maintaining the value.

305
00:36:48,560 --> 00:37:01,200
And so those were, that was a key, I think, realization for us was, you know, in some cases swapping things we already built that kind of worked for things that we knew would work for the next five or ten years as we increased the complexity was a huge unlock for us.

306
00:37:01,280 --> 00:37:07,520
And, you know, RevenueCat was great because we could integrate Stripe easier and stand up web in a day versus a month.

307
00:37:07,840 --> 00:37:09,680
And it'll be great when we go international because well.

308
00:37:09,760 --> 00:37:22,080
because we'll de-complexify a lot of these things that if we had been working in house would become, you know, a whole team we'd have to staff versus like a few lines of code we push or even configure within a module or a toggle set of toggles.

309
00:37:22,880 --> 00:37:39,760
So I would say those three things, understand your top of funnel and what drives it, really hone in on that first kind of session to seven day journey and then really understand buy versus build and make sure you have the right stuff in place to operate effectively would be my top three things that I would really recommend.

310
00:37:40,880 --> 00:37:53,920
Yeah, you've already kind of mentioned a couple of them, but what would you say are those top unlocks you've seen as far as buy versus build and bringing on third party tooling to help achieve that?

311
00:37:54,160 --> 00:38:06,320
I know, and we've talked about this before, how there is sometimes a little bit of internal engineering stigma around relying on third parties and like having third party SDKs in the app and things like that.

312
00:38:06,960 --> 00:38:14,960
So yeah, how do you how have you approached that build versus buy and then what specifically tools do you feel like have been the biggest unlockings for you?

313
00:38:15,680 --> 00:38:23,040
The best way to facilitate a build versus buy conversation is to show powerfully all the things you want to build that are on the roadmap but not yet built.

314
00:38:23,440 --> 00:38:35,520
Because if you have a lot of those, then when are these going to be built is kind of the question, and there's this implicit tension to basically time box these discussions around building versus buying.

315
00:38:36,080 --> 00:38:42,320
So that was a big thing for us is as we've kind of really enhanced the roadmap and had a really clear and convincing vision of where we're going and what we're building.

316
00:38:42,800 --> 00:38:50,720
Every line of code that isn't kind of driving that is like scrutinized and that I think really helps us as a company understand, hey, you know what?

317
00:38:51,280 --> 00:38:53,440
This is not going to be a strategic technology for us.

318
00:38:53,760 --> 00:38:56,240
There's off the shelf tools that work right out of the gate.

319
00:38:56,960 --> 00:39:07,040
This is probably worth it when we think about the value this same group of people can create focused on the things that are core to our competency, like personal safety and community safety.

320
00:39:08,000 --> 00:39:09,280
And so that's been really helpful.

321
00:39:09,840 --> 00:39:15,600
And then, yeah, I think to the extent that I mean, you can bring in tools like RevenueCat.

322
00:39:15,760 --> 00:39:23,600
I think a big unlock for us was as we had multi channel and really even multi platform, Apple and Android have different ways of defining the same metrics.

323
00:39:23,760 --> 00:39:33,280
It becomes a huge data engineering kind of hodgepodge of complexity to manage as to the definitions and how they work and how they operate.

324
00:39:33,680 --> 00:39:49,440
Integrating a set of industry standard definitions and allowing that to scale multi platform including web is just a really big unlock and decomplexifier for something that's very hard to in house given how complicated the various platforms have made sort of subscription management.

325
00:39:50,240 --> 00:39:58,880
And so that I think has been a really big unlock for us and added a lot of organizational clarity and reduced the trash of like, do we trust this metric or what have you?

326
00:39:59,680 --> 00:40:01,760
The other one that I kind of already mentioned was Superwall.

327
00:40:01,920 --> 00:40:06,080
I mean, Superwall and really modularizing our entire onboarding, which we did build in house.

328
00:40:06,320 --> 00:40:12,080
There's an incredible SaaS opportunity to just modularize onboardings and do what Superwall's doing for paywalls for onboardings.

329
00:40:12,320 --> 00:40:14,080
One day someone will start that and do very well.

330
00:40:14,800 --> 00:40:27,040
That was a huge unlock for us because it allowed us to move really quickly and kind of iterate on our highest kind of metric, one of our highest metric moving services in a very contained way that you just can't really do across your more mature user base.

331
00:40:27,840 --> 00:40:29,440
And Superwall was very core to that.

332
00:40:29,920 --> 00:40:35,600
The ability to push completely different paywalls or value props without writing really a line of code is a huge unlock.

333
00:40:36,400 --> 00:40:46,240
And testing pricing and different things on top of that system is just so much more leveraged and precise than it would be in a system in a world where you're building all your paywalls native.

334
00:40:46,800 --> 00:40:51,280
And then I think you also mentioned branch is something you rely on pretty heavily.

335
00:40:51,920 --> 00:40:52,000
Yeah.

336
00:40:52,320 --> 00:41:01,440
So, you know, we grow from content sharing and really to a lesser extent invitations and then obviously word of mouth, people talking about it, screenshotting, et cetera.

337
00:41:01,520 --> 00:41:05,360
And, and, and, and, and, and, and, and, and, and, you know, one of the primary ways we do attribution for that is actually through branch links.

338
00:41:05,520 --> 00:41:17,520
And so, um, if you're setting up, you know, uh, your kind of growth flows or your share flows, uh, highly recommend branch as a way of really simplifying attribution, but also giving you a lot of really useful customization.

339
00:41:17,840 --> 00:41:26,960
thumbnails, sharing thumbnails, et cetera that allow you to kind of really optimize something that seems almost like myopic in a way that's, you know, almost myopic in the short term.

340
00:41:27,040 --> 00:41:32,960
But, you know, when you zoom out and realize how these gains compound is an essential part of kind of accelerating your growth and your network effect.

341
00:41:33,440 --> 00:41:33,600
Yeah.

342
00:41:33,920 --> 00:41:34,000
Yeah.

343
00:41:34,240 --> 00:41:34,880
That's fantastic.

344
00:41:35,200 --> 00:41:40,000
And, you know, this is something you mentioned a little bit when we were talking with Jake in New York.

345
00:41:40,480 --> 00:41:59,520
Um, but when you're working on some of these minutia, when you're creating these deep links, when you're doing yet another AB test, keeping the team aligned on how all these little things kind of feed into the bigger mission can be a huge management challenge.

346
00:42:00,480 --> 00:42:10,880
So tell me a little bit more about how you kind of approach keeping the team aligned, even through the boring stuff, even through the annoying stuff, even through the like hundredth AB test.

347
00:42:12,160 --> 00:42:18,320
So it starts, I think, with clear goals and a culture of celebrating wins and creating kind of rapid feedback loops.

348
00:42:18,400 --> 00:42:24,320
And so for us, you know, we have goals that will necessitate a level of grinding it out, sort of tactical experimentation.

349
00:42:24,480 --> 00:42:25,760
Otherwise, you know, we won't meet them.

350
00:42:25,840 --> 00:42:29,280
Maybe that's an acquisition goal or a seven day activation goal or something.

351
00:42:29,600 --> 00:42:34,160
And so having a sharp kind of goal-oriented mindset and culture really helps.

352
00:42:34,320 --> 00:42:36,800
And then celebrating incremental progress helps a ton.

353
00:42:38,080 --> 00:42:46,720
I think other things that I've seen really work and really help are bringing in advisors or people who can evangelize even better than you can, you know, why these things really work.

354
00:42:47,200 --> 00:42:53,360
Shout out to Nikita Beer, very respected growth advisor who's kind of helping us actively right now rework some share flows and things.

355
00:42:53,600 --> 00:42:59,120
And you bring in folks like that to create kind of added momentum and also ensure you get even better best practices.

356
00:42:59,680 --> 00:43:08,800
And if you can't bring in advisors like that, the third thing I would say is really become a student of the game and understand what's working, what people are doing across industry.

357
00:43:09,200 --> 00:43:13,520
The top app charts are incredible because they're literally just a scoreboard of who's kind of winning.

358
00:43:13,920 --> 00:43:22,240
And all of their apps are available and you can kind of download them, you can screenshot them, you can understand put them in a Figma.

359
00:43:22,400 --> 00:43:31,680
We have a huge kind of library of just like different kind of teardowns of how different folks are kind of what flows look like for inspiration, for context, for clarification.

360
00:43:32,640 --> 00:43:43,440
And that instantly kind of raises the bar for us because if we don't kind of map to something that we've seen win in industry, well, the question is like, what are we inventing or why is it like, why isn't this good enough?

361
00:43:43,520 --> 00:43:43,600
Yeah.

362
00:43:44,320 --> 00:43:46,800
So those are a couple of things that I've seen really, really work for that.

363
00:43:47,120 --> 00:43:56,240
And I also think balancing teams across, you know, grinding it out and also kind of building, quote, unquote, more of the core value is important as well as kind of more of a stabilizing thing over time.

364
00:43:56,880 --> 00:43:58,880
Yeah, something you mentioned celebrating wins.

365
00:43:59,040 --> 00:44:05,440
Is there a specific citizen way that the product engineering in engineering celebrates a win?

366
00:44:06,240 --> 00:44:06,320
Yeah.

367
00:44:06,640 --> 00:44:12,080
So, you know, when we when we have an experimental write up that shows green, we send an email to our company.

368
00:44:12,800 --> 00:44:19,280
You know, we have kind of a culture of really retro wins and losses, but definitely wins a little bit more excitedly.

369
00:44:19,680 --> 00:44:30,080
And then that becomes part of, you know, the culture of just understanding the feedback loop of what's working and winning and often that will result in someone suggesting an idea that that win inspires or kind of a next thing to focus on.

370
00:44:30,480 --> 00:44:34,000
And you know, we have an all hands every two weeks, which is another kind of pedestal for that.

371
00:44:34,320 --> 00:44:36,960
And yeah, we take celebrating wins really seriously.

372
00:44:37,120 --> 00:44:40,320
I mean, product is very much and consumer tech is very much a team sport.

373
00:44:40,720 --> 00:44:45,920
And if you kind of you have to approach it kind of through the mindset, you're like building a team that's going to win a thing.

374
00:44:47,200 --> 00:44:51,520
And it's very different than, you know, building a feature that's going to achieve a blah or something.

375
00:44:52,800 --> 00:44:52,880
Yeah.

